<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rwandan Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hutu Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rwandan Revolution, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> Revolution, was a period of violence and political change in Rwanda from 1959 to 1961 that ended the Tutsi-led monarchy. Beginning with attacks on Tutsi in November 1959, it brought the Hutu party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmehutu">Parmehutu</a> to power with Belgian support, abolished the monarchy in 1961 and drove many Tutsi into exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rwandan-revolution.jpg" alt="Rwandan Revolution" width="256" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of Rwanda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stamp_of_Rwanda_-_1969_-_Colnect_588946_-_Worker_with_Pickaxe_and_Flag.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Rwanda had been ruled by a Tutsi monarchy since at least the 18th century. King Kigeli Rwabugiri, who reigned between 1853 and 1895, introduced ubuhake, under which Tutsi patrons gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> to clients in exchange for service, and uburetwa, forced labour for Tutsi chiefs, reforms that opened a rift between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> and Tutsi. German and then Belgian rule worked through the kings and supported Tutsi chiefs.</p><p>After 1945 a cash economy, a younger and more often Flemish clergy and seminary education allowed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> counter-elite to form; its best-known figure was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregoire-kayibanda">Grégoire Kayibanda</a>, editor of Kinyamateka and founder of the Mouvement Social Muhutu. Relations worsened from 1956, when sub-chief elections returned 66% Hutu while higher posts stayed overwhelmingly Tutsi. King Rudahigwa died in July 1959, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigeli-v-ndahindurwa">Kigeli V Ndahindurwa</a> was installed against Belgian wishes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 1 November 1959 nine members of the youth wing of UNAR, the Tutsi party, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominique-mbonyumutwa">Dominique Mbonyumutwa</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> sub-chief and PARMEHUTU activist, at Byimana. He survived, but rumours spread that he had been killed, and on 3 November Hutu vigilantes in Ndiza killed two Tutsi officials. Riots and arson against Tutsi homes spread across the country. Colonel Guy Logiest, a friend of the governor Jean-Paul Harroy, arrived on 4 November, restored order and appointed interim leaders chiefly from PARMEHUTU.</p><p>Communal elections in mid-1960 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> parties nearly all communes. After a National Reconciliation Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> failed in January 1961, Logiest and Kayibanda convened local leaders, who proclaimed a sovereign democratic Republic of Rwanda with Mbonyumutwa as interim president. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> report said an oppressive system had been replaced by another. PARMEHUTU won the legislature in September 1961, Kayibanda became president, and Rwanda became independent on 1 July 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By late 1964 an official total of 336,000 Tutsi had settled as refugees, chiefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>, Uganda, Tanganyika and Congo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a>. Some formed armed groups, called inyenzi, or cockroaches, by the government; after a surprise attack approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigali">Kigali</a> in late 1963, the government defeated the rebels and killed thousands of the Tutsi still in Rwanda. PARMEHUTU ruled unchecked until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juvenal-habyarimana">Juvénal Habyarimana</a>'s coup of 1973. In 1990 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rwandan-patriotic-front">Rwandan Patriotic Front</a>, formed largely of Tutsi refugees, invaded, and the civil war that followed led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rwandan Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tension between Tutsi and the Hutu emancipation movement broke into the 1959 <b>Rwandan Revolution</b>, in which Hutu activists killed Tutsi, burned their houses and drove more than 100,000 into exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
